// docs/spec/en/07-constexpr-and-consteval.md (ch09) §9.1(4)/(6): a
// `consteval` constructor is an immediate constructor, and it "may
// participate in required constant evaluation only for a class type
// satisfying 7.2(2)". `Box`'s only non-static data member is `int`
// (docs/spec/en/06-constant-evaluation.md (ch06) §7.2(1.1)), nothing is
// `mutable`, the constructor whose evaluation is required is itself
// `consteval`, and the destructor is compiler-provided -- so §7.2(2.1)
// through (2.4) are all satisfied.
// The namespace-scope `constexpr` initializer requires a constant expression
// ([dcl.constexpr]/6), so the constructor call cannot be deferred to run
// time; §7.2(6.2) requires the implementation to support it.
//
// Call position: namespace-scope initializer. Callee: `consteval`
// constructor.
//
// Expected value derived from the spec text above and independently confirmed
// with `clang++-22 -std=c++26`, which compiles this exact source and exits 42.
//
// CURRENT STATUS (scpp @ a59ffce): FAILS. scpp reports "error: global
// constructor-call initialization is not supported in this version". §7.2(6.2)
// nevertheless requires calls to `consteval` constructors to be supported
// during required constant evaluation, and a namespace-scope `constexpr`
// initializer is required constant evaluation. The expectation above comes
// from the spec and from clang; do not weaken it to match the implementation.
class Box {
public:
    virtual ~Box() = default;
public:
    int value{};

    consteval Box(int v) {
        this->value = v * 2;
        return;
    }
};

constexpr Box folded{21};

int main() {
    return folded.value;
}
